  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186115299,
      "id": "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac",
      "title": "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "81346c75-7f2c-5f91-8b23-cdb44b06deb5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+3y &=9 \\\\ 2x-y &=-3 \\\\ -4x+4y &=12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system has no solution",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 12\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y &=3 \\\\ -y &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=3$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=0.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cdba3de4-6dcb-5e84-8f91-83c65a484ad0",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
